D. Correct. Professional boundaries do not require rigid or emotionally distant treatment. Clinicians may appropriately demonstrate compassion, adapt to individual needs, and consider cultural context as long as their actions maintain role clarity, professional judgment, and a legitimate therapeutic purpose. The central question is not simply whether the therapist was flexible. It is whether the action benefited the client, preserved professional roles, and avoided dependency, confusion, impaired objectivity, or exploitation. Memory cue: Avoid answers suggesting that boundaries require either: Complete emotional distance, or Unlimited flexibility in the name of caring. The stronger answer usually combines warmth with role clarity and professional purpose.

